<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tibet</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tibet is a high plateau region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalayas</a>, with a distinct language and Buddhist culture. It was brought under Qing overlordship in the eighteenth century. The incorporation of Tibet, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>, defined the territorial framework later inherited by modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tibet.jpg" alt="Tibet" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dan · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%8B%B7%E8%B4%9D_(70097727).jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
